/* FMT 3: Identification */
Z("fmt3",0,"f",["Age is estimated from physical development, teeth (eruption and, in adults, regressive changes) and bones (appearance and fusion of ossification centres on X-ray), and in the elderly from degenerative changes; the medicolegal age is given as a range (e.g., 'between 18 and 20 years') because of biological variation.","Bones and teeth act as the body's calendar — centres appear and fuse, teeth erupt and wear, on a predictable timetable.",["Teeth: temporary (20) erupt 6 months–2.5 years; permanent (32) — first molar 6 yr, central incisor 7, lateral 8, first premolar 9, second premolar 10, canine 11, second molar 12, third molar 17–25 yr; adults — Gustafson's method.","Ossification: elbow fusion ~14–16 yr; lower end of radius ~18–19 yr; iliac crest ~20–22 yr; medial end of clavicle ~22–25 yr; sternum and skull sutures in later decades.","Other indicators: secondary sex characters, height, weight; pubic symphysis changes (Todd), skull suture closure, costal cartilage calcification, arcus senilis, osteoporosis in elderly.","Medicolegal importance: criminal responsibility (< 7 years — no offence, S. 20 BNS), juvenile (< 18 — JJ Act 2015), rape/POCSO (consent < 18), marriage (girl 18, boy 21), employment (child labour < 14), voting, criminal abortion, kidnapping, identification, attainment of majority, retirement."],"Age is always given as a range; 3 X-rays: elbow, wrist, pelvis."],
"Age estimation is an important part of identification of the living and the dead. Methods: (1) general physical development — height, weight, secondary sex characters (pubic and axillary hair at puberty, breast development, menarche ~12–14 years, voice change and beard ~15–17 years in boys); unreliable alone; (2) teeth (dental age) — most reliable up to about 25 years: temporary (deciduous, milk) teeth — 20 in number (2 incisors, 1 canine, 2 molars in each quadrant); eruption: lower central incisors 6–8 months, upper central incisors 7–9 months, lateral incisors 7–10 months, first molars 12–14 months, canines 16–20 months, second molars 20–30 months; calcification begins in the 4th–6th month of intrauterine life; shedding begins at 6–7 years; mixed dentition 6–12 years; permanent teeth — 32 (2 incisors, 1 canine, 2 premolars, 3 molars per quadrant); eruption: first molar 6–7 years (first permanent tooth, 'six-year molar'), central incisor 6–8, lateral incisor 7–9, first premolar 9–11, second premolar 10–12, canine 11–12, second molar 12–14 ('twelve-year molar'), third molar (wisdom tooth) 17–25 years; all permanent teeth except third molars erupted by 12–14 years; calcification of roots completes about 3 years after eruption; after 25 years, age is estimated from regressive changes (Gustafson's method — attrition, periodontosis, secondary dentine, cementum apposition, root resorption, root transparency); radiographs (OPG) show stages of crown/root formation (Demirjian method) in children; (3) ossification of bones (skeletal age) — the most reliable method from birth to 25 years: appearance of primary and secondary ossification centres and fusion of epiphyses with diaphyses, assessed by X-rays; standard radiographs requested for age estimation — elbow, wrist (with hand), shoulder, hip/pelvis, knee, and for older ages sternum, clavicle and skull; key landmarks (Indian data, girls about 1–2 years earlier than boys): at birth — centres of lower end of femur (appears 9th month IU — sign of maturity) and upper end of tibia (just before or after birth), cuboid; elbow — capitulum 1 year, head of radius 5–6, medial epicondyle 5–6, trochlea 9–10, olecranon 9–10, lateral epicondyle 10–11 years; fusion of elbow centres 14–16 years (first to fuse: lower end of humerus — 'elbow is the first'); wrist — carpal bones appear sequentially (capitate 1 year, hamate 1, triquetral 3, lunate 4, trapezium 5, trapezoid 6, scaphoid 6, pisiform 10–12 years — number of carpal bones roughly equals age up to about 7 years); lower end of radius fuses at 18–19 years, lower end of ulna 18–19; shoulder — head of humerus fuses 18–20 years; hip — head of femur, greater trochanter fuse 16–18 years, lesser trochanter 16–18; triradiate cartilage of acetabulum fuses 13–15 years; knee — lower end of femur and upper end of tibia fuse 17–19 years; iliac crest appears 14–15 and fuses 20–22 years; ischial tuberosity fuses 20–22; medial (sternal) end of clavicle appears 18–20 and fuses 22–25 years (last epiphysis to fuse); sternum — body segments fuse 15–25 years, xiphoid with body ~40 years, manubrium with body after 60 years; skull — anterior fontanelle closes 18–24 months, posterior 2–3 months; sutures close from inside: sagittal 30–40 years, coronal 30–40, lambdoid 40–50, squamous after 60 years (unreliable); basi-occiput fuses with basi-sphenoid 18–22 years; hyoid — greater cornua fuse with body 40–60 years; (4) other methods in adults/elderly — pubic symphysis metamorphosis (Todd's ten phases, Suchey–Brooks method) from 18 to 50+ years — reliable in middle age; sternal end of fourth rib (Iscan's phases); auricular surface of ilium; calcification of laryngeal and costal cartilages (from 30–40 years); lipping and osteophytes of vertebrae, osteoporosis, loss of teeth with absorption of alveoli, mandible changes (angle obtuse in infants and old age, ~110–120° in adults), arcus senilis, grey hair, wrinkled skin, cataract; histological methods (osteon counting), aspartic acid racemisation in dentine, DNA methylation (epigenetic clock). Report: the doctor examines with consent (court-ordered examination as needed), records findings with X-ray numbers, and opines age as a range (e.g., 'above 16 and below 18 years'); courts generally accept a margin of error of ±2 years (in favour of the accused in juvenile determination — JJ Act 2015 S. 94: first matriculation certificate, birth certificate, and only in their absence ossification test). Medicolegal importance of age: (a) criminal responsibility — nothing is an offence done by a child under 7 years (S. 20 BNS / 82 IPC); child above 7 and under 12 who has not attained sufficient maturity of understanding (S. 21 BNS / 83 IPC); (b) Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act 2015 — child < 18; 16–18 years accused of heinous offences may be tried as adult after preliminary assessment; (c) rape and sexual offences — sexual intercourse with a girl under 18 is rape irrespective of consent (S. 63 BNS); POCSO Act (child < 18); enhanced punishment for victim < 12 or < 16; (d) kidnapping — from lawful guardianship of a minor (boy < 16, girl < 18 — S. 137 BNS); (e) marriage — Prohibition of Child Marriage Act 2006 (girl 18, boy 21); (f) employment — Child Labour Amendment Act 2016 (no child < 14 in any occupation; adolescents 14–18 not in hazardous work); Factories Act; (g) criminal abortion and MTP (consent of guardian if < 18); (h) civil — attainment of majority (18 years; 21 if guardian appointed by court), competency to make a will, contract, vote (18), consent for medical treatment (18), adoption, inheritance; (i) identification of an individual or remains; (j) service — joining (age limits) and superannuation; (k) infanticide (newborn — viability, maturity); (l) sports and pension disputes.",
[["Eruption of teeth",[["Teeth","Temporary","Permanent"],["Central incisor","6–8 months","6–8 years"],["Lateral incisor","7–10 months","7–9 years"],["Canine","16–20 months","11–12 years"],["First premolar","—","9–11 years"],["Second premolar","—","10–12 years"],["First molar","12–14 months","6–7 years"],["Second molar","20–30 months","12–14 years"],["Third molar","—","17–25 years"]]],
["Ossification landmarks (approx., Indian)",[["Region","Event","Age"],["Elbow","All centres fused","14–16 yr"],["Hip","Head of femur, trochanters fused","16–18 yr"],["Knee","Lower femur, upper tibia fused","17–19 yr"],["Wrist","Lower radius/ulna fused","18–19 yr"],["Shoulder","Head of humerus fused","18–20 yr"],["Iliac crest","Fused","20–22 yr"],["Medial clavicle","Fused (last)","22–25 yr"],["Skull","Sutures close","30–60 yr"]]],
["Medicolegal importance",["Criminal responsibility (< 7 yr: S. 20 BNS)","Juvenile (< 18, JJ Act 2015)","Rape/POCSO (< 18)","Kidnapping, child marriage, child labour","Majority, consent, vote, will","Identification, service, pension"]]],
{i:"Age estimation combines dental, skeletal and general findings and is expressed as a range.",x:[["Adult/elderly indicators",["Gustafson's dental changes","Pubic symphysis (Todd/Suchey–Brooks)","Sternal rib end (Iscan)","Skull sutures, cartilage calcification","Osteoporosis, osteophytes, arcus senilis"]]],f:"Birth–14 yr: teeth eruption + appearance of centres\n 14–25 yr: fusion of epiphyses (elbow → hip → knee → wrist → shoulder → iliac crest → clavicle) + third molar\n > 25 yr: Gustafson, pubic symphysis, sutures, degenerative changes",c:"Age is estimated from teeth (eruption up to 25 years and Gustafson's changes thereafter), ossification centres on X-rays (most reliable till 25 years) and degenerative changes in older adults, always expressed as a range, and it determines criminal responsibility, juvenile status, consent and many civil rights."});

Z("fmt3",1,"f",["Sex of skeletal remains is best determined from the pelvis (most reliable; ~95% accuracy alone) followed by the skull (~90%); the female pelvis is broad, shallow and light with a wide subpubic angle (> 90°), wide sciatic notch and preauricular sulcus, while the male skull is larger and rugged with prominent supraorbital ridges, square orbits with rounded margins, large mastoids and square chin.","The female pelvis is built for childbirth (wide and roomy); the male skull is built bigger and rougher for muscle attachment.",["Pelvis (male vs female): heavy, rough vs light, smooth; inlet heart-shaped vs oval/round; cavity deep funnel vs shallow wide; subpubic angle V-shaped 70–75° vs U-shaped 90–100°; greater sciatic notch narrow, deep vs wide, shallow; obturator foramen oval vs triangular; acetabulum large vs small; preauricular sulcus absent vs present; ischiopubic index < 90 vs > 90; sacrum long narrow, curved vs short wide, flat.","Skull: male larger, heavier, capacity ~1450 vs 1300 ml; prominent supraorbital ridges, glabella; sloping forehead vs vertical; orbits square, rounded upper margins vs round with sharp margins; mastoid large; external occipital protuberance prominent; mandible — square chin, everted gonial angle (< 125°), high ramus vs rounded chin, inverted angles.","Other bones: femur (head diameter > 47.5 mm male, < 43.5 female), sternum (body > twice manubrium in male — Hyrtl's law), humerus head, atlas; long bones heavier, muscle markings prominent in males.","Accuracy: entire skeleton ~98–100%, pelvis + skull 98%, pelvis 95%, skull 90%, long bones 80%; sex in soft tissue — external genitalia, Barr body (sex chromatin), Y-chromosome (F-body), DNA (amelogenin AMELX/AMELY), intersex conditions."],"Pelvis > skull for sex; subpubic angle: male V < 90°, female U > 90°."],
"Determination of sex is a primary step in identification. In a living person or fresh body, sex is evident from external genitalia, secondary sex characters (breasts, hair distribution, voice, body contour), and internal genitalia (uterus, prostate — which resist decomposition longest among soft tissues); in doubtful cases (intersex, mutilation, decomposition) — nuclear sexing (Barr body/sex chromatin in buccal smear or hair root sheath cells — present in females; 'drumsticks' in neutrophils), fluorescent Y-chromatin (F-body) in males, karyotyping, and DNA analysis (amelogenin gene — AMELX on X gives 106 bp and AMELY on Y gives 112 bp fragment; SRY gene). In skeletal remains, sex differences become marked only after puberty; prepubertal sexing is unreliable. Accuracy (Krogman): entire skeleton — 100%; pelvis and skull — 98%; pelvis alone — 95%; skull alone — 90%; long bones alone — 80%. Pelvis (most reliable bone because of adaptation to childbirth): male pelvis — massive, heavy, rough with marked muscle markings; false pelvis deep; pelvic inlet (brim) heart-shaped (android) with prominent sacral promontory; pelvic cavity deep and funnel-shaped (conical); pelvic outlet small; subpubic angle narrow, V-shaped, 70–75° (approximating the angle between the index and middle fingers); pubic arch narrow; body of pubis narrow, triangular; ischiopubic rami everted (with rough crest for crus of penis); greater sciatic notch narrow and deep (~ 50–60°, accepts the index finger only); obturator foramen large and oval; acetabulum large (diameter ~ 52 mm), directed laterally; ischial tuberosities inverted, closer together; preauricular sulcus usually absent; sacrum long, narrow, with more pronounced curvature and promontory, body of S1 large (> 1/3 of base width — 'corporobasal index'); ischiopubic index (pubic length × 100 / ischial length) < 90 (≈ 73–94); sciatic notch index; female pelvis — light, smooth, delicate; false pelvis shallow; inlet oval or round (gynaecoid); cavity shallow and wide (cylindrical); outlet large; subpubic angle wide, U-shaped, 90–100° (angle between extended thumb and index finger); pubic arch broad; body of pubis broad, quadrangular; ischiopubic rami slightly everted and smoother; greater sciatic notch wide and shallow (~ 75°, accepts two fingers); obturator foramen small and triangular; acetabulum small (~ 46 mm), directed anterolaterally; ischial tuberosities everted, farther apart; preauricular sulcus well-marked (attachment of anterior sacroiliac ligament; deepened by pregnancy — 'scars of parturition' on dorsal pubic surface); sacrum short, wide, flatter, with less curvature, body of S1 small (< 1/3 of base); ischiopubic index > 90 (≈ 91–115). Skull (second most reliable): male skull — larger, heavier, thicker, with greater capacity (~ 1450–1500 ml vs ~ 1300 ml in female), rugged with well-marked muscle ridges (temporal lines, nuchal lines); forehead sloping (receding); frontal and parietal eminences less prominent; supraorbital ridges (brow ridges) and glabella prominent; frontonasal junction distinctly angulated; orbits square-shaped, placed lower, relatively smaller, with rounded (blunt) upper margins; zygomatic arches more prominent and laterally bowed; mastoid processes large and prominent (skull rests on mastoids when placed on a table); external occipital protuberance prominent (inion hook); occipital condyles large and long; palate large, broad, U-shaped; teeth larger; mandible — larger, thicker, with square chin, high symphysis, broad ascending ramus, everted and prominent gonial angle (< 125°), condyles large; female skull — smaller, lighter, smoother, more rounded and infantile; forehead vertical, rounded with prominent frontal and parietal eminences; supraorbital ridges and glabella less prominent or absent; frontonasal junction smoothly curved; orbits rounded, placed higher, relatively larger, with sharp upper margins; zygomatic arches less prominent; mastoid processes small; external occipital protuberance less marked; palate small, parabolic; mandible — smaller, with rounded/pointed chin, gonial angle less prominent, inverted (> 125°), narrow ramus. Other bones: sternum — in males the body is more than twice the length of the manubrium (Hyrtl's law; female — less than twice), total length > 149 mm male; sternal index; clavicle longer, heavier in males; femur — vertical diameter of head > 47.5 mm in males, < 43.5 mm in females (Pearson); bicondylar width, length > 459 mm male; neck–shaft angle smaller in females (wider pelvis); humerus head vertical diameter > 47 mm male, < 43 female; scapula larger; atlas, calcaneus, talus measurements; discriminant function analysis using multiple measurements. Medicolegal importance: identification of unknown bodies and skeletal remains (mass disasters, mutilated remains), establishing identity in crime, intersex cases (marriage, inheritance, sports eligibility, legitimacy, employment), sexual offences, impotence and sterility questions.",
[["Pelvis: male vs female",[["Feature","Male","Female"],["General","Heavy, rough, muscle markings","Light, smooth"],["Inlet","Heart-shaped","Oval/round"],["Cavity","Deep, funnel-shaped","Shallow, wide"],["Subpubic angle","V-shaped, 70–75°","U-shaped, 90–100°"],["Greater sciatic notch","Narrow, deep","Wide, shallow"],["Obturator foramen","Large, oval","Small, triangular"],["Acetabulum","Large (~52 mm)","Small (~46 mm)"],["Preauricular sulcus","Absent/faint","Well marked"],["Sacrum","Long, narrow, curved","Short, wide, flat"],["Ischiopubic index","< 90","> 90"]]],
["Skull: male vs female",[["Feature","Male","Female"],["Size/capacity","Large, ~1450 ml","Small, ~1300 ml"],["Forehead","Sloping","Vertical, rounded"],["Supraorbital ridges/glabella","Prominent","Less/absent"],["Orbits","Square, low, rounded margins","Round, high, sharp margins"],["Mastoid","Large","Small"],["Occipital protuberance","Prominent","Less marked"],["Chin","Square","Rounded/pointed"],["Gonial angle","Everted, < 125°","Inverted, > 125°"]]],
["Accuracy of sexing (Krogman)",[["Material","Accuracy"],["Entire skeleton","100%"],["Pelvis + skull","98%"],["Pelvis alone","95%"],["Skull alone","90%"],["Long bones","80%"]]],
["Other methods",["Hyrtl's law (sternum)","Femoral head diameter","Barr body, F-body","DNA: amelogenin, SRY","Prostate/uterus resist decomposition"]]],
{c:"The pelvis is the most reliable bone for sexing (95%), with the female showing a wide U-shaped subpubic angle, wide sciatic notch and preauricular sulcus, followed by the skull (90%), where males show larger size, prominent brow ridges, square orbits, large mastoids and a square chin; DNA and nuclear sexing help in doubtful cases."});

Z("fmt3",2,"f",["Dactylography (fingerprint system, Galton system) is the study of fingerprints for identification; papillary ridge patterns — loops (~65–70%), whorls (~25%), arches (~5%) and composites (~1–2%) — are unique to each individual (even identical twins), permanent from about the 4th month of intrauterine life till decomposition, and classified by the Henry system; identity is established by matching ridge characteristics (minutiae), usually 8–16 points.","Fingerprints are nature's barcode — no two people, not even identical twins, share the same ridge pattern, and it never changes.",["Patterns: loops (radial/ulnar — most common), whorls, arches (plain, tented — least common), composites (central pocket loop, double loop, lateral pocket, accidental); core and delta points.","Basis: individuality and permanence; ridges formed by 4th month IU; destroyed only by deep injury to dermis (scar), leprosy, radiation, decomposition; Henry classification (1897, India — Bengal; first fingerprint bureau Kolkata 1897).","Lifting prints: patent (visible), latent (invisible — developed with powders, ninhydrin for amino acids on paper, iodine fumes, silver nitrate, cyanoacrylate superglue fuming, laser/ALS), plastic (impressed in soft material); AFIS (Automated Fingerprint Identification System); Aadhaar biometrics.","Uses: identification of criminals, unknown dead, mass disasters, deserters, impersonation; signatures of illiterates (thumb impression), passport, banking; poroscopy (Locard — sweat pores), ridgeoscopy, edgeoscopy; foot and palm prints; lip prints (cheiloscopy)."],"Loops > whorls > arches; Henry system; 12-point (8–16) matching."],
"Dactylography (Greek 'dactylos' — finger; also called dermatoglyphics, Galton system, fingerprint system) is the study of the patterns of papillary ridges on the fingertips for the purpose of identification; it is the most reliable method of personal identification (other than DNA). History: Johann Purkinje (1823) described patterns; William Herschel (1858, Bengal) used thumb impressions on contracts; Henry Faulds (1880) proposed use for criminal identification; Francis Galton (1892) proved individuality and permanence, described minutiae (Galton details); Sir Edward Richard Henry with Azizul Haque and Hem Chandra Bose devised the Henry system of classification (1897, Bengal); the world's first fingerprint bureau was established at Writers' Building, Calcutta (Kolkata) in 1897; Central Fingerprint Bureau, now under NCRB, Delhi. Anatomy: papillary (friction) ridges are formed by dermal papillae projecting into the epidermis; sweat pores open along the ridges; ridges develop from about the 12th week and are complete by the 4th month (16 weeks) of intrauterine life; they remain unchanged throughout life except for increase in size with growth, and persist after death until decomposition destroys the skin (can be recovered from degloved skin or by special techniques even in early decomposition, mummified fingers rehydrated). Principles: (1) individuality — no two persons, including monozygotic twins, have identical fingerprints (probability of two identical prints ~ 1 in 64 billion); also prints of the ten fingers of the same person differ; (2) permanence — patterns do not change during life; superficial injuries, burns, abrasions heal with regeneration of the same pattern; permanent alteration only when the dermis (papillary layer) is destroyed — deep cuts, burns, scars, certain diseases (leprosy, scleroderma, acanthosis), radiation dermatitis, manual labour (temporary wear), chemotherapy (capecitabine — adermatoglyphia), congenital absence (adermatoglyphia); attempts at mutilation by criminals leave scars which are themselves identifying; (3) classifiability — patterns can be systematically classified for filing and retrieval. Fingerprint patterns (Galton–Henry): (1) loops — about 65–70% (most common); ridges enter from one side, recurve and exit on the same side; one delta and one core; radial loop (opens towards the thumb/radius) and ulnar loop (opens towards the little finger — more common); (2) whorls — about 25–30%; ridges form circles, spirals or ovals around a central core; two deltas; types — plain whorl, central pocket loop whorl, double loop whorl, accidental whorl; (3) arches — about 5–6% (least common); ridges enter from one side and exit on the other, rising in the centre; no delta (plain arch) or a tented arch with a central upthrust; (4) composites — about 1–2% — combinations (central pocket loop, lateral pocket loop, twinned loop, accidental). Ridge characteristics (minutiae, Galton details) — ridge ending, bifurcation, lake (enclosure), island (short ridge, dot), spur, crossover, bridge, delta, core; identification requires concordance of a sufficient number of characteristics in the same relative positions without unexplained differences — conventionally 12 points in many countries (8–16; India commonly 8–12; some now use a holistic non-numerical standard); ridge counting and ridge tracing used for classification. Henry classification: based on the presence of whorls in the ten fingers (numerical values assigned to fingers in pairs — 16, 8, 4, 2, 1), giving a primary classification as a fraction (1/1 to 32/32 = 1024 primary groups), with secondary and sub-secondary classification; now largely replaced by computerised AFIS (Automated Fingerprint Identification System) and in India by CCTNS/NAFIS (National Automated Fingerprint Identification System, NCRB 2022). Types of prints at a crime scene: (a) patent (visible) prints — made by fingers contaminated with blood, ink, paint, grease, dirt; photographed directly; (b) latent (invisible) prints — formed by sweat and sebaceous secretions (amino acids, fats, salts) on surfaces; developed by — powders (aluminium, black/grey powder, magnetic powder, fluorescent powders) on non-porous surfaces; chemical methods on porous surfaces like paper — ninhydrin (reacts with amino acids → Ruhemann's purple), DFO, iodine fumes (temporary brown), silver nitrate (reacts with chloride → black under light); cyanoacrylate (superglue) fuming for non-porous surfaces, plastics; small particle reagent for wet surfaces; vacuum metal deposition; alternate light sources and lasers; (c) plastic (impressed) prints — on soft materials like wax, soap, putty, butter, wet paint; photographed and cast; prints are lifted with adhesive tape or photographed; comparison with records (specimen ten-print card taken with printer's ink, inkless/livescan systems). Fingerprinting of the dead: prints taken after cleaning, softening (in rigor — rolling fingers after flexing joints; in decomposition — removing the glove of skin and wearing it over the examiner's gloved finger; injecting glycerine/water to fill out wrinkled tips; mummified fingers soaked in sodium hydroxide/softening solutions). Other related methods: poroscopy (Locard, 1912 — study of the shape, size and arrangement of sweat pores on ridges — useful with fragmentary prints); ridgeoscopy/edgeoscopy (shape of ridge edges); palm prints, sole prints (footprints of newborns in hospitals to prevent baby exchange), lip prints (cheiloscopy — Suzuki classification), ear prints, nail ridges. Uses (medicolegal importance): identification of criminals and habitual offenders (Identification of Prisoners Act 1920, replaced by Criminal Procedure (Identification) Act 2022 — measurements including finger impressions, palm prints, footprints, photographs, iris and retina scans, biological samples); identification of unknown dead bodies, mutilated bodies, victims of mass disasters; detection of impersonation (examinations, elections, pension, property transactions); thumb impressions as signatures of illiterate persons on documents (wills, deeds, dying declarations); passports, visas, immigration; banking, Aadhaar biometric authentication, access control; identification of newborns, missing persons, deserters, persons with amnesia; scientific evidence linking a suspect to a crime scene or weapon; dermatoglyphic patterns associated with genetic disorders (Down syndrome — simian crease, ulnar loops, high axial triradius; Turner syndrome; Klinefelter).",
[["Fingerprint patterns",[["Pattern","Frequency","Features"],["Loops (ulnar/radial)","~65–70%","Enter and exit same side; 1 delta, 1 core"],["Whorls","~25–30%","Circular/spiral; 2 deltas"],["Arches (plain/tented)","~5%","Enter one side, exit other; no delta"],["Composites","~1–2%","Combinations, accidentals"]]],
["Developing latent prints",[["Method","Surface"],["Powders (aluminium, magnetic, fluorescent)","Non-porous"],["Ninhydrin (amino acids → purple)","Paper/porous"],["Iodine fumes","Paper (temporary)"],["Silver nitrate (chlorides)","Paper"],["Cyanoacrylate fuming","Plastics, non-porous"],["Lasers/alternate light","Various"]]],
["Uses",["Identification of criminals, unknown dead, disaster victims","Detection of impersonation","Thumb impression signature of illiterates","Passports, Aadhaar, banking","Newborn identification (footprints)","Dermatoglyphics in Down syndrome"]],
["Related methods",["Poroscopy (Locard)","Ridgeoscopy/edgeoscopy","Cheiloscopy (lip prints)","Palm and sole prints","AFIS/NAFIS"]]],
{c:"Dactylography identifies individuals by their unique, permanent fingerprint ridge patterns — loops, whorls, arches and composites — classified by the Henry system and matched by minutiae; latent prints are developed with powders, ninhydrin or cyanoacrylate, and fingerprints serve criminal, civil and disaster identification."});

Z("fmt3",3,"f",["Teeth are the hardest and most durable tissues of the body, resisting decomposition, fire and trauma, and help identify individuals through dental records, dental formula, restorations and peculiarities, estimate age by eruption (till 25 years) and by Gustafson's six regressive changes after 25 years, and link suspects through bite marks (forensic odontology).","Teeth are the body's 'black box' — they survive fire and decay, and dental records match them like a fingerprint.",["Dental formula: temporary 20 — i 2/2, c 1/1, m 2/2 × 2 = 20; permanent 32 — I 2/2, C 1/1, PM 2/2, M 3/3 × 2 = 32; notation — FDI two-digit system (11–48 permanent, 51–85 deciduous), Palmer, Universal.","Gustafson's method (1950) — six changes, each scored 0–3: Attrition (A), Periodontosis/paradentosis (P), Secondary dentine (S), Cementum apposition (C), Root resorption (R), Root transparency (T — most reliable); total points → age by regression (age = 11.43 + 4.56 × total); error ± 3.6 years. Mnemonic 'APS CRT'.","Identification: comparing antemortem dental records/X-rays with postmortem findings — fillings, crowns, bridges, dentures (may carry name), missing teeth, rotation, diastema, malocclusion, Hutchinson's teeth (congenital syphilis), mottled enamel (fluorosis), tobacco/pan staining, occupational notching (tailors, carpenters).","Bite marks: in sexual assaults, child abuse, fights — photographed with scale, swabbed for saliva DNA, impressions/casts compared with suspect's dentition; teeth also indicate race (shovel incisors — Mongoloids; Carabelli cusp — Caucasians), habits, socioeconomic status."],"Gustafson 'APS CRT': Attrition, Periodontosis, Secondary dentine, Cementum, Root resorption, Transparency."],
"Forensic odontology is the branch of forensic medicine dealing with the proper handling, examination and evaluation of dental evidence in the interest of justice. Teeth are valuable because enamel is the hardest substance in the body, highly resistant to decomposition, putrefaction, chemicals and heat (teeth survive temperatures up to ~ 1000 °C, though they may crack and shrink), so teeth often remain when other identifying features are lost (burnt, decomposed, skeletonised, mutilated bodies, mass disasters such as aircraft crashes and tsunamis); also every person's dentition (with its restorations and peculiarities) is practically unique. Dental formula: temporary (deciduous) dentition — 20 teeth: in each half of each jaw — 2 incisors, 1 canine, 2 molars: formula i 2/2, c 1/1, m 2/2 = 5/5 × 2 = 20; permanent dentition — 32 teeth: in each half of each jaw — 2 incisors, 1 canine, 2 premolars, 3 molars: formula I 2/2, C 1/1, PM 2/2, M 3/3 = 8/8 × 2 = 32; premolars replace deciduous molars; permanent molars have no deciduous predecessors. Dental notation systems: FDI (Fédération Dentaire Internationale) two-digit system — first digit indicates quadrant (1 upper right, 2 upper left, 3 lower left, 4 lower right for permanent; 5–8 for deciduous), second digit tooth (1 central incisor to 8 third molar; e.g., 36 = lower left first molar); Palmer (Zsigmondy) notation — quadrant symbols with numbers 1–8; Universal numbering (USA) — 1–32 starting from upper right third molar. Dental identification: (1) comparative identification — post-mortem dental chart and radiographs of the unknown are compared with ante-mortem dental records (charts, radiographs including OPG, casts, photographs, treatment notes) of the presumed person; points compared — number of teeth present, missing teeth (extracted antemortem — healed socket, or postmortem loss — empty sharp socket), restorations (amalgam fillings, composite, gold inlays, crowns, root canal treatment, bridges, implants — with shape and radiographic appearance), dentures (may be marked with patient's name/ID — denture marking recommended), orthodontic appliances, carious lesions, fractures, rotations, malposition, crowding, spacing (diastema), supernumerary teeth, impacted teeth, root morphology, sinus outline, trabecular pattern; even a single unique restoration can establish identity; (2) reconstructive post-mortem dental profiling when antemortem records are unavailable — estimation of age, sex (limited — canine dimensions, DNA from pulp), race/ancestry (shovel-shaped upper incisors in Mongoloids/Asians; Carabelli's cusp on upper first molar in Caucasians; large teeth/bimaxillary prognathism), occupation and habits (notched incisors in tailors, cobblers, carpenters holding nails/pins; pipe smokers' wear; musicians' wear; tobacco, pan and betel staining; bruxism), socioeconomic status (quality of dental care), diet, systemic diseases and conditions (Hutchinson's incisors — notched, peg-shaped in congenital syphilis; mulberry (Moon's) molars; mottled enamel in fluorosis; tetracycline staining; enamel hypoplasia; erosion in bulimia/occupational acid exposure), drug abuse (methamphetamine caries); (3) DNA from dental pulp — well protected source of DNA in decomposed/burnt remains; (4) superimposition of smile photographs. Age estimation by teeth: (a) in children and adolescents — by eruption and calcification of temporary and permanent teeth (most reliable till 14 years; third molar till ~ 25 years); radiographic stages (Demirjian 8-stage method, Schour and Massler charts); neonatal line in enamel indicates live birth (visible microscopically when the infant has survived some days after birth); incremental lines; (b) in adults (> 25 years) — Gustafson's method (Gösta Gustafson, 1950) based on six age-related regressive changes in a longitudinal ground section of a tooth, each graded 0 to 3 (0 — no change, 1 — slight, 2 — moderate, 3 — marked): (1) attrition (A) — wearing of the occlusal/incisal surface due to mastication (from enamel only to dentine to pulp); (2) periodontosis/paradentosis (P) — regression of the gums and periodontal tissues with exposure of roots (from beginning to more than two-thirds of root); (3) secondary dentine deposition (S) — deposition within the pulp cavity, progressively narrowing it; (4) cementum apposition (C) — increase in thickness of cementum at the root, especially near the apex; (5) root resorption (R) — resorption of the root surface (cementum and dentine) at the apex; (6) root transparency (T) — dentine of the root becomes transparent from the apex upwards due to deposition of minerals in dentinal tubules (considered the most reliable single criterion); total points = A + P + S + C + R + T; age is read from a regression line (Gustafson: age = 11.43 + 4.56 × total points; standard error ± 3.6 years; later modifications by Johanson, Maples, Bang & Ramm root transparency method, Lamendin method — periodontosis + transparency); disadvantages — tooth must be extracted and sectioned, subjective grading, wide error in elderly, variation by tooth type and population. Other dental age methods in adults: cementum annulation (incremental lines), aspartic acid racemisation in dentine, pulp/tooth area ratio on radiographs (Kvaal, Cameriere), periodontal changes. Bite marks: patterned injuries produced by teeth on skin or objects (food — cheese, fruit, chewing gum); seen in sexual assaults (breasts, neck, thighs, genitals — in sexual sadism), child abuse (battered baby), assaults and fights, self-inflicted (on forearm); features — two opposing arcs of contusions/abrasions (upper arch wider), central suck mark (ecchymosis), individual tooth marks (shape, spacing, rotation, missing teeth); evidence collection — swab for salivary DNA (double swab technique — wet then dry; amylase), photography with ABFO no. 2 scale perpendicular to mark (colour, black-and-white, UV, IR), impressions/casts of the mark (silicone, plaster) and tissue excision in dead bodies (fixed in formalin with ring support), and suspect's dental casts and bite exemplars (in wax) for comparison (overlays, digital comparison); exclusion is more reliable than positive identification (concerns about scientific validity of bite-mark analysis — NAS 2009, PCAST 2016 reports); bite marks may also indicate time (healing changes) and whether antemortem. Medicolegal importance of teeth: identification of individuals (living and dead, mass disasters — DVI using INTERPOL forms), age estimation (criminal responsibility, juvenile status, sexual offences), sex and race determination, linking assailants via bite marks, evidence of occupation/habits/disease, as weapons (bite injuries — grievous hurt if tooth fractured; human bite infections), dental negligence cases, poisoning (lead line on gums — Burton's line, mercury, arsenic, phosphorus jaw necrosis), and fracture/dislocation of a tooth constitutes grievous hurt (S. 116 BNS).",
[["Dental formula",[["Dentition","Formula","Total"],["Temporary","i 2/2, c 1/1, m 2/2","20"],["Permanent","I 2/2, C 1/1, PM 2/2, M 3/3","32"]]],
["Gustafson's method",[["Change","Description","Score"],["Attrition (A)","Wear of occlusal surface","0–3"],["Periodontosis (P)","Gum and periodontal regression","0–3"],["Secondary dentine (S)","Narrowing of pulp cavity","0–3"],["Cementum apposition (C)","Thickening at root","0–3"],["Root resorption (R)","Apical resorption","0–3"],["Root transparency (T)","Transparent apical dentine (most reliable)","0–3"],["Formula","Age = 11.43 + 4.56 × total","± 3.6 yr"]]],
["Identification features",["Restorations: fillings, crowns, bridges, RCT, implants","Dentures (name marking)","Missing/extracted teeth, rotation, diastema","Hutchinson's teeth, mottled enamel, staining","Occupational notching","Racial traits: shovel incisors, Carabelli cusp","Pulp DNA"]],
["Bite marks",["Sexual assault, child abuse, fights","Saliva swab (DNA)","Photography with ABFO scale","Impressions/casts; compare with suspect","Exclusion more reliable than inclusion"]]],
{c:"Teeth resist decomposition and fire, making them invaluable for identification through comparison with dental records, for age estimation by eruption up to 25 years and Gustafson's six regressive changes thereafter, and for linking suspects through bite marks, all within forensic odontology."});

Z("fmt3",4,"f",["DNA fingerprinting (DNA profiling, Alec Jeffreys 1985) identifies individuals by analysing highly variable regions of DNA — originally VNTRs by RFLP, now short tandem repeats (STRs) amplified by PCR (CODIS 20 core loci) — whose combination is unique to each person except identical twins; it is used in paternity disputes, identification of remains and sexual assault and other crime investigations.","Each person's DNA has repeating 'stutters' of different lengths at many sites; reading lengths at 15–20 sites gives a code virtually unique to that person.",["Sources: blood, semen (vaginal swabs — differential extraction separates sperm DNA), saliva, hair with root (nuclear DNA; shaft — mtDNA), bone, teeth (pulp), tissue, nails, touch DNA; preserved dry or frozen; EDTA blood; avoid formalin.","Techniques: RFLP (VNTR, Southern blot — large quantities, slow), PCR-based STR typing (multiplex kits, capillary electrophoresis — current standard; amelogenin for sex), Y-STR (male lineage; sexual assault mixtures), mitochondrial DNA (maternal lineage; degraded samples, hair shafts, bones), SNPs, next-generation sequencing; DNA databases (CODIS, India — DNA Technology Regulation Bill pending).","Paternity: child gets one allele from each parent at each locus — exclusion if child's allele absent in alleged father at ≥ 2–3 loci; inclusion with paternity index/probability > 99.99%; maternity, sibship, immigration cases.","Uses and issues: sexual assault, homicide, identification of mass disaster/mutilated remains, exonerating wrongly convicted, missing persons, organ transplant chimerism; limitations — contamination, mixtures, degradation, identical twins, privacy; legal — S. 51/52 BNSS (DNA sampling of accused), BSA expert opinion; Supreme Court — no compulsory DNA paternity test without strong prima facie case (protects legitimacy, S. 116 BSA / 112 IEA)."],"STR + PCR = modern DNA profiling; mtDNA = maternal; Y-STR = paternal line."],
"DNA fingerprinting (DNA profiling, DNA typing) is a technique to identify an individual on the basis of the unique pattern of his DNA; it was developed by Sir Alec Jeffreys (University of Leicester, 1984–85); first forensic use — Colin Pitchfork case (1987, England) — the first conviction based on DNA evidence and the first exoneration of a suspect; in India — developed at CCMB Hyderabad (Lalji Singh, 1988, 'father of Indian DNA fingerprinting'); CDFD (Centre for DNA Fingerprinting and Diagnostics), Hyderabad. Principle: more than 99.9% of human DNA is identical between individuals; the variation lies in polymorphic regions, especially non-coding repetitive sequences — (a) variable number tandem repeats (VNTRs, minisatellites) — repeat units of 10–100 base pairs repeated many times; (b) short tandem repeats (STRs, microsatellites) — repeat units of 2–6 bp (usually tetranucleotide), repeated 5–50 times; the number of repeats at each locus varies among individuals (alleles), and each person inherits one allele from the mother and one from the father at each locus; by examining multiple independent loci (13 original CODIS core loci, now 20; Indian kits use 15–24 loci) the combined probability of two unrelated persons sharing the same profile (random match probability) becomes astronomically small (1 in billions–trillions); identical (monozygotic) twins share the same nuclear DNA profile (cannot be distinguished by STRs). Sources (biological evidence): blood (white cells; fresh liquid in EDTA or dried stains), semen and seminal stains (sperm heads rich in DNA; azoospermic semen has epithelial cells), vaginal/anal/oral swabs in sexual assault (mixed samples — differential extraction separates sperm DNA from victim's epithelial DNA), saliva (buccal cells — swabs, bite marks, cigarette butts, envelopes, cups), hair with roots (follicular tissue — nuclear DNA; shed hair shafts without root — mitochondrial DNA), tissues and organs, bone (femur/long bone cortex — in decomposed or skeletonised remains), teeth (dental pulp — well protected), fingernail scrapings (assailant's tissue), urine and faeces (few cells), sweat and skin cells ('touch DNA' on handled objects), products of conception and fetal tissue (paternity in rape-induced pregnancy). Collection and preservation: wear gloves and masks, avoid contamination; air-dry wet stains and pack in paper envelopes (not plastic — mould degrades DNA); blood in EDTA vials refrigerated; tissues frozen; avoid formalin fixation (degrades DNA); swabs dried; control samples from victim and suspect (buccal swab or blood); maintain chain of custody; label and seal. Techniques: (1) RFLP (restriction fragment length polymorphism) analysis — original Jeffreys method; DNA extracted, digested by restriction enzymes (e.g., HinfI), fragments separated by agarose gel electrophoresis, transferred to nylon membrane (Southern blotting), hybridised with radiolabelled probes for VNTR loci (multi-locus or single-locus probes), visualised by autoradiography as a bar-code-like band pattern; requires large amount (~ 50–100 ng to µg) of good-quality, non-degraded DNA; slow (weeks); now obsolete; (2) PCR (polymerase chain reaction, Kary Mullis 1983) — amplifies specific DNA regions from minute quantities (< 1 ng; even a few cells) and degraded samples; (3) STR typing — current standard: multiplex PCR amplification of multiple STR loci with fluorescent-labelled primers, separation of products by capillary electrophoresis on automated genetic analysers, sizing against an allelic ladder, producing an electropherogram with peaks at each locus; the profile is a set of numbers (repeat counts) — easily compared and stored in databases; amelogenin locus included for sex (X 106 bp, Y 112 bp); commercial kits — AmpFlSTR Identifiler (15 loci + amelogenin), GlobalFiler (21 loci + DYS391 + Y indel + amelogenin), PowerPlex Fusion; (4) Y-chromosome STR (Y-STR) — male-specific, inherited intact from father to son (paternal lineage — shared by all male relatives); useful in sexual assault mixtures with excess female DNA, multiple assailants, paternity of male children, missing persons; (5) mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA) analysis — sequencing of hypervariable regions (HV1, HV2) of the control region (D-loop); mtDNA present in hundreds–thousands of copies per cell (so survives in degraded/old samples, hair shafts, bones, teeth); inherited only from the mother (maternal lineage — siblings and maternal relatives share same mtDNA); used in identification of skeletal remains (e.g., Romanov family), mass disasters, old samples; less discriminating; heteroplasmy; (6) SNP (single nucleotide polymorphism) analysis — useful for degraded DNA, ancestry and phenotype (eye/hair colour) prediction; (7) next-generation sequencing (massively parallel sequencing), rapid DNA instruments; (8) X-STRs for complex kinship. Interpretation: match (inclusion — profiles identical at all loci; statistical weight given by random match probability/likelihood ratio using population allele frequencies), exclusion (profiles differ — conclusive), inconclusive (insufficient or mixed data); in paternity testing — at each locus the child must have one allele from the mother (obligate maternal allele) and the other (obligate paternal allele) must be present in the alleged father; exclusion if mismatches at ≥ 2–3 loci (single mismatch may be mutation); inclusion — combined paternity index and probability of paternity (> 99.9–99.99% considered practically proved). Applications (medicolegal uses): (A) criminal — identification of the assailant in rape, sexual assault (semen, saliva, hair), murder (blood stains, tissue under nails), linking crime scenes (serial offenders), databases (CODIS in USA, NDNAD in UK); exonerating the innocent (Innocence Project — hundreds of wrongful convictions overturned); (B) civil — disputed paternity and maternity (maintenance, inheritance, legitimacy, baby swapping in hospitals, immigration and citizenship, surrogacy and ART disputes); (C) identification — unknown dead bodies, mutilated or decomposed remains, mass disaster victims (DVI — 2004 tsunami, plane crashes; compared with relatives' reference samples or personal items like toothbrushes), missing persons, soldiers (Tandoor case, Nithari case, Aarushi), historical identification; (D) others — organ and bone marrow transplant matching and chimerism monitoring, wildlife forensics, food adulteration, medical diagnosis of genetic disorders. Advantages: highly discriminating, applicable to small/old/degraded samples, objective numerical results, databases. Limitations: contamination and mixed samples, degradation (heat, humidity, UV, bacteria), PCR inhibitors, identical twins indistinguishable by STR, chimerism (after bone marrow transplant blood DNA becomes donor's), interpretation errors, lab quality issues (requires accredited labs, NABL/ISO 17025), cost, ethical and privacy concerns (genetic data, familial searching), court admissibility (expert evidence S. 39 BSA). Legal aspects in India: DNA evidence admissible as expert opinion (S. 39 BSA / 45 IEA); BNSS S. 51 (53 CrPC explanation — 'examination' includes DNA profiling and other modern techniques) and S. 52 (53A — examination of rape accused including DNA profiling) permit collection of samples from the accused (reasonable force may be used); BNSS S. 184 (164A) — victim examination includes collection of material for DNA profiling with consent; Criminal Procedure (Identification) Act 2022 — biological samples; DNA Technology (Use and Application) Regulation Bill 2019 (withdrawn 2023) — proposed national DNA data bank and regulatory board; Supreme Court — courts cannot order DNA tests for paternity as a matter of routine; strong prima facie case and eminent need required, considering the conclusive presumption of legitimacy for a child born during a valid marriage (S. 116 BSA / 112 IEA — rebuttable only by proving non-access) (Goutam Kundu v. State of West Bengal 1993; Nandlal Wasudeo Badwaik v. Lata 2014 — DNA test result prevails over presumption when conducted; Rohit Shekhar v. N.D. Tiwari 2012 — DNA paternity established; Ashok Kumar v. Raj Gupta 2021 — right to privacy); Selvi (2010) — DNA sampling is not testimonial compulsion (not violating Article 20(3)).",
[["Techniques",[["Method","Target","Features"],["RFLP","VNTRs (minisatellites)","Southern blot; large good-quality DNA; obsolete"],["PCR-STR","STRs (2–6 bp repeats)","Multiplex, capillary electrophoresis; standard; small samples"],["Y-STR","Y chromosome","Paternal lineage; sexual assault mixtures"],["mtDNA","HV1/HV2 of D-loop","Maternal lineage; degraded samples, hair shafts, bone"],["SNP / NGS","Single nucleotide variants","Degraded DNA, ancestry, phenotype"]]],
["Sources of DNA",["Blood, semen, vaginal swabs","Saliva, bite marks, cigarette butts","Hair with root (nuclear) / shaft (mtDNA)","Bone, teeth (pulp)","Nail scrapings, touch DNA","Fetal tissue (rape pregnancy paternity)"]],
["Applications",[["Field","Uses"],["Criminal","Rape, murder, linking crimes, exoneration"],["Civil","Paternity, maternity, inheritance, immigration, baby swap"],["Identification","Unknown dead, mutilated remains, mass disasters"],["Medical","Transplant chimerism, genetic diagnosis"]]],
["Limitations",["Contamination, mixtures","Degradation, inhibitors","Identical twins","Chimerism after BMT","Privacy, lab quality"]]],
{c:"DNA fingerprinting identifies individuals by their unique pattern of polymorphic DNA repeats, now analysed by PCR-based STR typing with Y-STR and mitochondrial DNA for special situations; it is applied in sexual assault and homicide investigation, paternity disputes and identification of remains, subject to careful collection, contamination control and legal safeguards."});

Z("fmt3",5,"f",["Identification of mutilated or fragmentary remains aims to establish whether the parts are human, whether they belong to one or more individuals, and the age, sex, stature, race, time since death, cause of death and individual identity — using anatomy, precipitin test for species, bone measurements (stature from long bones by Karl Pearson/Trotter–Gleser formulas), dental comparison and DNA.","Like solving a jigsaw: first prove the pieces are human, then decide how many people they came from, then build the profile — age, sex, height — and finally match to a name.",["Questions: human or animal (anatomy, histology — Haversian canal diameter, precipitin test with anti-human serum), one or more individuals (duplication of parts, size, age, sex mismatch, fitting of joints), sex, age, stature, race, identity marks, time since death, cause and manner of death, whether dismembered antemortem or postmortem, tool used (saw marks).","Stature: whole body length + 2–2.5 cm in dead (vs living); from long bones — Pearson's and Trotter–Gleser regression formulas (femur most reliable; femur ≈ 27% of height, humerus ≈ 20%, tibia ≈ 22%, radius ≈ 14%); arm span ≈ height; head–trunk; foot length ≈ 15%.","Individual identity: tattoos, scars, deformities, moles, healed fractures, surgical implants (serial numbers on pacemakers, joint prostheses), dental records, fingerprints, DNA (compare with relatives), superimposition of skull on photograph, facial reconstruction, clothing and belongings.","Dismemberment: criminal (to conceal identity/facilitate disposal) vs accidental (train, blast, animals); antemortem cuts show vital reaction; disarticulation at joints suggests anatomical knowledge; parts identified and matched; mass disasters — DVI protocol (INTERPOL: AM/PM data — fingerprints, dental, DNA primary identifiers)."],"Primary identifiers (INTERPOL DVI): fingerprints, dental, DNA."],
"Mutilated bodies, fragmentary remains and skeletal remains are encountered in homicides with dismemberment (to conceal identity and facilitate disposal), railway and road accidents, explosions and blast injuries, aircraft crashes, fires and burns, building collapses and natural disasters (earthquakes, floods, tsunamis), animal depredation (dogs, jackals, fish, crabs, rodents) and advanced decomposition. The medicolegal examination aims to answer: (1) whether the remains are human or animal — gross anatomy of bones (human long bones have characteristic shapes; skull and pelvis distinctive); histology (human bone — Haversian systems with larger, fewer canals arranged irregularly; animal bone — plexiform bone, smaller canals); if soft tissue/blood present — precipitin test (Uhlenhuth; antigen–antibody reaction with anti-human serum), species-specific DNA; hair microscopy (medullary index < 1/3 in humans); (2) whether the parts belong to one or more individuals — check for duplication of parts (two right femurs indicate at least two persons — minimum number of individuals), consistency of size, age, sex, race, colour, degree of decomposition, articulation of joint surfaces, matching of cut margins of soft tissue and bone, blood groups and DNA profiles; (3) sex — external/internal genitalia, prostate, uterus (resist putrefaction), pelvis, skull, long bone dimensions, nuclear sexing and DNA (amelogenin); (4) age — teeth, ossification centres and epiphyseal fusion, pubic symphysis, skull sutures, degenerative changes, Gustafson's method; (5) stature — (a) if body is complete — measure crown–heel length; dead body length exceeds living height by about 2–2.5 cm (relaxation of muscles, loss of tone, loss of curvatures); (b) from body parts — arm span (distance between middle fingertips with arms outstretched) approximately equals height; distance from vertex to symphysis pubis is about half the height; from sternal notch to symphysis × 3.3; head to chin ≈ 1/8 of height; foot length ≈ 15%; hand length; (c) from long bones — the most reliable method uses regression formulas developed from bone lengths (dry or fresh) — Karl Pearson's formula (1899, for Europeans), Trotter and Gleser (1952, for American whites and blacks — most widely used), and Indian formulas (Pan's, Nath's, Siddiqui and Shah, Mehta); multiplication factors (approximate, Indian): femur × 3.7 (femur ≈ 27% of height), tibia × 4.5 (22%), humerus × 5 (20%), radius × 6.9 (14%), ulna × 6.3 (16%), fibula × 4.4; lower limb bones give better estimates than upper; formulas differ for sex and population; add 2.5 cm if fresh bones used with dry bone formulas (cartilage) or vice versa; (6) race/ancestry — skull (cephalic index: dolichocephalic < 75 — pure Aryans, Aborigines; mesaticephalic 75–80 — Europeans, Indians; brachycephalic > 80 — Mongoloids), nasal aperture, orbits, prognathism, incisors (shovel-shaped in Mongoloids), hair characteristics, skin colour; brachial and crural indices; DNA ancestry markers; (7) personal identification — features individualising the person: congenital or acquired deformities (club foot, polydactyly, kyphosis), scars (surgical, burn, vaccination, operation scars — e.g., appendicectomy, caesarean), tattoos (may be visible in decomposed skin with infrared/removing epidermis; regional lymph nodes contain pigment), moles, birthmarks, occupational marks (calluses), circumcision, healed fractures with callus and orthopaedic implants (plates, screws, nails, joint prostheses — often with manufacturer's serial number traceable to the patient), pacemakers, breast implants, intrauterine devices, dental work and dentures; dental comparison with antemortem records; fingerprints (even from degloved or mummified skin); DNA profiling (bone, teeth, tissue) compared with reference samples from relatives (parents, children, siblings — kinship analysis) or personal items (toothbrush, hairbrush, razor); radiological comparison (antemortem X-rays — frontal sinus pattern is unique, vertebral features, trabecular pattern); photographic superimposition (skull–photo superimposition — Buck Ruxton case 1935, the classical case of dismemberment in which Dr. Ruxton's wife and maid were identified by superimposition of skulls on photographs and by maggot age), video superimposition, forensic facial reconstruction (soft-tissue depth markers — Gerasimov, Manchester method; digital 3D); clothing, jewellery, wallets, identity documents, mobile phones (useful but circumstantial — can be planted); (8) time since death — state of decomposition, insect (entomological) evidence, adipocere, mummification, state of bones (smell, fat, marrow, ligaments, cartilage — fresh bones with tags of soft tissue < 1 year; weathering; ultraviolet fluorescence of fresh bone — lost with age; nitrogen content, amino acids, benzidine reaction), plant roots growing through remains; (9) cause and manner of death — injuries on remaining parts (fractures, cut marks, bullet holes, ligature marks, skull fractures), toxicology (hair, bone, maggots for poisons), whether injuries antemortem (vital reactions — haemorrhage, swelling, retraction of edges, infiltration) or postmortem (dismemberment usually postmortem), type of instrument used for dismemberment (saw marks — kerf, striations; knife cuts at joints suggest anatomical knowledge, e.g., butcher, doctor; axe/chopper marks; railway injuries — grease and soot, crush and amputation); (10) scene examination — recovery of all parts systematically (grid search), animal scavenging patterns, burial. Mass disaster victim identification (DVI): INTERPOL DVI protocol — phases: scene (recovery with labelling), post-mortem data collection (pink forms — fingerprints, dental, DNA, physical description), ante-mortem data collection from families (yellow forms — medical/dental records, DNA reference samples, photographs), reconciliation (matching by identification board) and debriefing; primary identifiers — fingerprints, comparative dental analysis and DNA (each sufficient alone); secondary identifiers — personal description, medical findings, tattoos, property, clothing (supportive). Report: the expert gives opinion on each question with reasons and limitations; parts preserved for further tests; bodies handed over after identification with dignity.",
[["Questions in fragmentary remains",[["Question","Methods"],["Human or animal?","Anatomy, histology (Haversian canals), precipitin test, DNA"],["One or more individuals?","Duplication, size, age, sex mismatch, joint fit, DNA"],["Sex","Pelvis, skull, genitalia, prostate/uterus, DNA"],["Age","Teeth, ossification, pubic symphysis, sutures"],["Stature","Body length (+2–2.5 cm dead), long bone formulas"],["Race","Cephalic index, nasal aperture, teeth"],["Identity","Scars, tattoos, implants, dental, fingerprints, DNA, superimposition"],["Time since death","Decomposition, insects, bone condition"],["Cause/manner","Injuries, vital reaction, tool marks, toxicology"]]],
["Stature from long bones (approx. multiplication factor)",[["Bone","Factor","% of height"],["Femur","× 3.7","27%"],["Tibia","× 4.5","22%"],["Humerus","× 5","20%"],["Radius","× 6.9","14%"],["Ulna","× 6.3","16%"]]],
["DVI (INTERPOL)",["Phases: scene, PM data, AM data, reconciliation, debriefing","Primary identifiers: fingerprints, dental, DNA","Secondary: personal description, medical, property, tattoos"]],
["Classic case",["Buck Ruxton (1935): dismemberment; skull–photo superimposition; maggot age for time since death"]]],
{c:"Mutilated remains are examined systematically to determine whether they are human, how many individuals are represented, and the age, sex, stature (from long bones by regression formulas), race, personal identity, time since death and cause of death, with fingerprints, dental comparison and DNA serving as primary identifiers."});
